  thing.

  My proposal: dismissal is recorded per [1m[38;5;153m(taskId, kind)[0m, persisted, and [1mre-arms[0m when either

  1. the task's [38;5;153mdueAt[39m changes (you rescheduled it — it's a new commitment), or
  2. the alert escalates [38;5;153mdue-soon → overdue[39m (a genuinely new fact).

  So dismissing "due soon" still lets the overdue alert through later; dismissing "overdue" silences that task until you
  reschedule it. Completing the task clears its dismissals.

  [1mData flow.[0m One direction, no reactive framework:

  load() ──> tasks ──┬──> renderTaskList
                     └──> computeAlerts(tasks, now) ──> renderAlertBanner

  mutation (add/edit/complete/dismiss) ──> update tasks ──> save() ──> re-render both
  timer (60s) ──> recompute alerts with fresh now ──> re-render banner only

  The 60-second tick only recomputes alerts; it never touches storage or re-renders the task list, so it can't clobber
  something you're typing.

  [1mWhy 60 seconds:[0m it bounds how stale an alert can be, and the cost is a pure function over a small array. A [38;5;153msetTimeout[39m
  scheduled exactly at the next [38;5;153mdueAt[39m would be more elegant but breaks when a laptop sleeps through the wake time.
